---
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import ContributionFlow from '../components/ContributionFlow.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import PageVisual from '../components/PageVisual.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../lib/paths';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Contribute"
  description="How to join genomeOS, choose Ready work, use GitHub Issues and Projects, and open a reviewable contribution."
>
  <PageIntro
    note="Open collaboration"
    title="Find work that fits how you think."
    lede="The project is organized so a literature researcher, statistician, engineer, designer, clinician, or community partner can find a bounded task and understand why it matters."
  />

  <PageVisual
    image={sitePath('/images/contribute-community.webp')}
    alt="Contributors around the world bringing research, code, data, design, and discussion into a shared atlas"
    kicker="Many ways to contribute"
    title="Bring the perspective the atlas is missing."
    description="Local knowledge, overlooked literature, scientific critique, modeling, infrastructure, design, and community experience all make the resource more complete and more useful."
  />

  <ContributionFlow />

  <section class="contribution-detail">
    <div class="section-shell contribution-detail__grid">
      <article>
        <h2>1. Start with people</h2>
        <p>
          Introduce yourself in <a
            href="https://github.com/genomeOS/genomeOS/discussions/76"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Discussion #76</a
          >. Share the disciplines, languages, datasets, places, or product
          questions you know. You do not need to arrive with a finished
          proposal.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h2>2. Use issues as the record</h2>
        <p>
          Search <a
            href="https://github.com/genomeOS/genomeOS/issues?q=is%3Aissue"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">open and closed issues</a
          >
          before starting. Open issues are the work queue. Closed issues preserve
          decisions and house patterns—including ideas deliberately declined.
        </p>
        <p>
          When posting a substantive update, mention the original issue author
          so the person who framed the work is notified.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h2>3. Read board status literally</h2>
        <dl class="status-definitions">
          <div>
            <dt>Backlog</dt><dd>
              Logged, but not yet specified for implementation.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Ready</dt><dd>
              Fully specified and unblocked; take it without asking.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>In progress</dt><dd>Someone is actively moving the issue.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>In review</dt><dd>
              A pull request or expert review is underway.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Blocked</dt><dd>
              A named dependency or decision prevents progress.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h2>4. Filter by four label families</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <code>type:*</code> describes the kind of contribution: data, science,
            infrastructure, user interface, documentation, governance, or outreach.
          </li>
          <li>
            <code>P*:</code> is the project board’s shorthand for a part of the system:
            population locations, measured evidence, frequency modeling, disease-burden
            modeling, the data service, or the interactive map. You do not need to
            memorize the codes.
          </li>
          <li>
            <code>skill:*</code> helps you find work matching your expertise.
          </li>
          <li>
            <code>priority:*</code> follows the dependency graph—what breaks if work
            is late—not how exciting it sounds.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h2>5. Respect decision and review labels</h2>
        <p>
          <code>wants-expert-review</code> means implement the issue, then state exactly
          what a domain expert should check.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>needs-human-decision</code> means stop at the choice: surface the
          evidence and options, but do not commit on the project’s behalf.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h2>6. Make the pull request auditable</h2>
        <p>
          Connect the scientific objective, acceptance evidence, engineering
          interface, assumptions, refusal conditions, and downstream users. Link
          the issue with the correct closing keyword and report every
          verification run honestly.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="validation-section">
    <div class="section-shell">
      <Callout title="Milestones are release boundaries" tone="aurora">
        <p>
          The first milestone establishes the data foundation; the second
          reproduces published haemoglobin S science; the third makes the map
          usable; and the fourth makes public launch safe and understandable.
          Issues belong to both a part of the system and a release milestone
          because those answer different questions.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="resource-links">
    <div class="section-shell">
      <h2>Open the live workspace</h2>
      <div class="resource-links__grid">
        <a
          href="https://github.com/orgs/genomeOS/projects/1"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          ><strong>Project board</strong><span
            >See status, priority, milestones, and ownership.</span
          ></a
        >
        <a
          href="https://github.com/genomeOS/genomeOS/issues"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          ><strong>Issues</strong><span
            >Search decisions and choose a scoped task.</span
          ></a
        >
        <a
          href="https://github.com/genomeOS/genomeOS/pulls"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          ><strong>Pull requests</strong><span
            >Learn from active implementation and review.</span
          ></a
        >
        <a
          href="https://github.com/genomeOS/genomeOS/issues/65"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          ><strong>Community standards</strong><span
            >Follow the contribution-file work tracked in Issue #65.</span
          ></a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
